+---
+id: task-001-stable-task-identifiers
+type: task
+title: "Stable task identifiers"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+# Stable task identifiers
+
+## Request
+
+The app needs a stable way to refer to a task, one that still works after the task is renamed. Each
+task gets a unique identifier.
+
+- `add` returns the task with an `id`: a string that no other task of the list has.
+- The list gets a `get(id)` method that returns the task with that id, as a result like the list's
+  other methods (`Result<Task>`, with `not-found` when there is no such task).
+- The methods that take a title (`find`, `complete`, `reopen`, `rename`, `tag`, `untag`, `remove`)
+  also accept a task's id.
+
+## Traceability
+
+- Feature: task list for the Tidy Tasks app (`README.md`).
+- Requirement: a task keeps its identity across renames.
+- Acceptance: see the request above; covered by tests in `test/list.test.ts`.
+
+## Design
+
+- `Task` gets a readonly `id: string`, assigned by `createTask` when the task is created. The id is
+  `task-<n>`, where `n` is a counter the list advances on each successful `add`. No randomness is used,
+  as the domain directive r3 requires. The counter only moves forward, so an id is never reused after
+  a removal, and it is unique within the list.
+- The list keeps tasks keyed by id. Titles stay unique; `add` and `rename` refuse a title another task
+  has.
+- `get(id)` looks up by id only and fails with `not-found` for an unknown id.
+- The other methods take a reference (`ref`): it is looked up as an id first, then as a title. Ids
+  are checked first so that an id always works; a title that happens to equal another task's id
+  would then reach that task instead.
+- `rename` keeps the id and changes only the title.
+- `remove` removes the task by id.
+
+## Notes
+
+- No credentials or secrets are involved.
+- Public API change: `Task` gains `id`; `TaskList` gains `get`. The new `get` and the `id` field are
+  additive for existing callers.
